1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,135
A command from this lecture's README,
on a laptop that never built this image:

2
00:00:05,135 --> 00:00:08,427
with no local image,
it pulls one from Docker Hub.

3
00:00:08,747 --> 00:00:14,997
Inside, Python says 3.8.17,
and the Gemini library won't import.

4
00:00:15,233 --> 00:00:19,030
Yet this repo's Dockerfile says Python 3.14.

5
00:00:19,350 --> 00:00:24,684
This image was built in February 2025,
with Python 3.8.

6
00:00:25,004 --> 00:00:26,220
Where did it come from?

7
00:00:26,467 --> 00:00:32,398
A second surprise, from the other tutorial:
a server listening on port 9200.

8
00:00:32,718 --> 00:00:34,673
The laptop can't reach it;

9
00:00:34,673 --> 00:00:38,584
another container,
asking by name, gets a reply: ok.

10
00:00:38,833 --> 00:00:39,857
Two puzzles.

11
00:00:40,177 --> 00:00:44,145
Part 1: where did that image come from,
and should you build or pull?

12
00:00:44,465 --> 00:00:46,620
Part 2: whose localhost is it?

13
00:00:46,867 --> 00:00:51,390
The tracker shows the README step,
code panels show file and line numbers.

14
00:00:51,710 --> 00:00:53,968
Pause at the yellow your-turn tag;

15
00:00:53,968 --> 00:00:57,555
gray extra tags mark commands
the README doesn't have.

16
00:00:57,800 --> 00:01:00,339
Every command is also in the study guide.

17
00:01:00,659 --> 00:01:05,430
Part 1 needs Tutorial
0 and a Google Cloud project with billing;

18
00:01:05,430 --> 00:01:07,293
part 2 needs only Docker.

19
00:01:07,533 --> 00:01:09,338
We recorded on Linux,

20
00:01:09,338 --> 00:01:13,720
with Docker Engine 29.8.2
at the time of recording.

21
00:01:14,040 --> 00:01:18,008
On a Mac or in Git BASH,
the prompt and a few messages differ;

22
00:01:18,008 --> 00:01:19,864
the commands and ports don't.

23
00:01:20,100 --> 00:01:23,023
One honest limit: we had no Google account.

24
00:01:23,343 --> 00:01:26,601
Wherever the code calls Google,
you'll see the command,

25
00:01:26,601 --> 00:01:29,977
the real error a missing key gives,
and what to look for.

26
00:01:31,017 --> 00:01:34,275
In lecture 3,
the course handed you a shared bucket

27
00:01:34,275 --> 00:01:35,489
and one shared key,

28
00:01:35,489 --> 00:01:37,694
copied into every component folder.

29
00:01:38,014 --> 00:01:42,494
This branch is the same pipeline,
built the way a real team would build it.

30
00:01:42,734 --> 00:01:44,441
Your team owns its bucket.

31
00:01:44,761 --> 00:01:49,155
You create your own service account,
and keep its one key outside the repo.

32
00:01:49,400 --> 00:01:51,989
You no longer write the Dockerfile:

33
00:01:51,989 --> 00:01:56,205
it's committed,
together with pyproject.toml and uv.lock.

34
00:01:56,525 --> 00:02:00,111
And each component has one script,
docker-shell.sh,

35
00:02:00,111 --> 00:02:04,120
that holds the long docker build
and docker run commands.

36
00:02:04,367 --> 00:02:08,533
The five stages haven't changed,
and they still never call each other:

37
00:02:08,533 --> 00:02:11,748
each writes to the bucket,
and the next reads from it.

38
00:02:12,068 --> 00:02:14,970
Containers
that talk directly come in part 2.

39
00:02:15,217 --> 00:02:17,350
A team can get an image two ways.

40
00:02:17,670 --> 00:02:21,638
Early in a project,
everyone builds it from the recipe in Git.

41
00:02:21,958 --> 00:02:25,776
Later, one person publishes
finished images to Docker Hub,

42
00:02:25,776 --> 00:02:27,356
and everyone else pulls.

43
00:02:27,600 --> 00:02:29,029
Look closely at the tag.

44
00:02:29,349 --> 00:02:31,890
It's a signpost to one particular image,

45
00:02:31,890 --> 00:02:35,131
built on some day,
from some version of the recipe.

46
00:02:35,451 --> 00:02:38,224
Nothing promises it matches
the commit you have.

47
00:02:38,467 --> 00:02:42,648
One script decides which of the two you get;
we'll read it line by line.

48
00:02:42,968 --> 00:02:44,120
First, the code.

49
00:02:44,367 --> 00:02:45,519
Starting fresh?

50
00:02:45,839 --> 00:02:50,614
From your workspace folder,
clone the repo with this branch checked out:

51
00:02:50,614 --> 00:02:53,135
git clone, --branch flexible-workflow.

52
00:02:53,455 --> 00:02:55,546
Then cd into mega-pipeline.

53
00:02:55,866 --> 00:02:57,167
Pause and run both.

54
00:02:57,400 --> 00:02:58,684
Two extra checks:

55
00:02:58,684 --> 00:03:03,294
git branch shows flexible-workflow
at the commit we recorded,

56
00:03:03,294 --> 00:03:06,427
and transcribe audio holds its Dockerfile,

57
00:03:06,427 --> 00:03:11,352
the script, pyproject.toml and uv.lock,
which this branch commits.

58
00:03:11,600 --> 00:03:13,819
Already have your clone from lecture 3?

59
00:03:14,139 --> 00:03:17,637
It's on main; if you cloned fresh,
skip to the next chapter.

60
00:03:17,957 --> 00:03:19,856
Step 1: cd into it.

61
00:03:20,176 --> 00:03:22,160
Two extra checks show its state.

62
00:03:22,867 --> 00:03:23,955
Three piles.

63
00:03:24,275 --> 00:03:26,728
Your edited cli.py is tracked.

64
00:03:27,048 --> 00:03:30,142
Your two new files
in generate text are untracked.

65
00:03:30,462 --> 00:03:35,414
In transcribe audio,
the Dockerfile, pyproject.toml, uv.lock

66
00:03:35,414 --> 00:03:37,395
and the key are ignored.

67
00:03:37,634 --> 00:03:39,661
Step 2, git fetch origin.

68
00:03:39,981 --> 00:03:41,365
Here it prints nothing:

69
00:03:41,365 --> 00:03:45,399
a clone already has every branch,
and nothing had changed upstream.

70
00:03:45,719 --> 00:03:47,298
Yours may list updates.

71
00:03:47,534 --> 00:03:52,504
Step 3, git checkout flexible-workflow,
and Git refuses, for two reasons.

72
00:03:52,750 --> 00:03:55,620
Your edits to cli.py would be overwritten,

73
00:03:55,620 --> 00:04:00,131
and two untracked files sit
where the branch has files of its own.

74
00:04:00,451 --> 00:04:01,625
Nothing changed.

75
00:04:01,867 --> 00:04:04,528
The README's note covers the first reason:

76
00:04:04,528 --> 00:04:08,139
git stash puts your edits away,
and the first wall falls.

77
00:04:08,459 --> 00:04:11,339
On a retry,
the untracked files still block it.

78
00:04:11,584 --> 00:04:14,784
A small gap:
the note covers only edited files.

79
00:04:15,104 --> 00:04:18,581
An extra git stash -u
stashes untracked files too,

80
00:04:18,581 --> 00:04:20,181
and the checkout works.

81
00:04:20,501 --> 00:04:23,159
The other option, git checkout -- .,

82
00:04:23,159 --> 00:04:27,072
discards your edits and leaves
untracked files alone.

83
00:04:27,317 --> 00:04:30,752
Step 5, git branch: * flexible-workflow.

84
00:04:31,072 --> 00:04:36,054
Before you switch your own clone,
copy out any lecture 3 Dockerfile,

85
00:04:36,054 --> 00:04:41,184
pyproject.toml and uv.lock you want to keep:
the switch replaces them.

86
00:04:41,504 --> 00:04:42,698
Pause and switch.

87
00:04:42,934 --> 00:04:46,768
Extra checks show
your ignored lecture 3 Dockerfile,

88
00:04:46,768 --> 00:04:50,971
pyproject.toml and uv.lock
were replaced by the branch's,

89
00:04:50,971 --> 00:04:54,880
without a word:
copy them out first if you want them.

90
00:04:55,117 --> 00:04:58,406
The old key still sits
inside transcribe audio;

91
00:04:58,406 --> 00:05:01,346
your secrets stay put, as the README says.

92
00:05:01,666 --> 00:05:04,243
Every build there copies it into the image,

93
00:05:04,243 --> 00:05:07,000
so delete
each component's secrets folder now.

94
00:05:07,320 --> 00:05:09,026
Your edits wait in the stash.

95
00:05:10,550 --> 00:05:13,434
Before you run, the README's folder layout:

96
00:05:13,434 --> 00:05:17,995
your key lives in a secrets folder
beside the repo, never inside it.

97
00:05:18,315 --> 00:05:21,643
Go up one level, to your workspace,
and create that folder.

98
00:05:24,083 --> 00:05:28,118
Step 2:
every cli.py sets three values near the top:

99
00:05:28,118 --> 00:05:31,998
your project ID, your bucket,
and your group name.

100
00:05:32,233 --> 00:05:35,771
An extra grep shows what ships:
three files are blank,

101
00:05:35,771 --> 00:05:41,144
but generate text and translate text already
hold the course's project and bucket,

102
00:05:41,144 --> 00:05:43,241
and the instructor's group name.

103
00:05:43,483 --> 00:05:47,648
A small slip: the README says
an assert stops you if you forget,

104
00:05:47,648 --> 00:05:49,926
but it only rejects an empty value.

105
00:05:50,246 --> 00:05:54,251
Those two files would send your calls
to the course's project and bucket,

106
00:05:54,251 --> 00:05:56,390
where they fail with permission errors.

107
00:05:56,633 --> 00:05:58,916
Set all three, in all five files.

108
00:05:59,236 --> 00:06:02,089
An extra git diff shows our placeholders,

109
00:06:02,089 --> 00:06:06,404
your-project-id, your-team-bucket
and group-01; type your own.

110
00:06:06,724 --> 00:06:07,918
In translate text,

111
00:06:07,918 --> 00:06:11,566
also change the target language
from Spanish to French.

112
00:06:11,886 --> 00:06:13,209
Pause and edit them.

113
00:06:13,450 --> 00:06:17,798
Step 3, your bucket: the components read
and write these prefixes,

114
00:06:17,798 --> 00:06:20,895
and every output lands
under your group's name.

115
00:06:21,133 --> 00:06:22,494
Another small gap:

116
00:06:22,494 --> 00:06:26,048
the README never says how
to create the bucket,

117
00:06:26,048 --> 00:06:29,026
and its sample upload is named test.mp3.

118
00:06:29,346 --> 00:06:31,267
Create the bucket in the console.

119
00:06:31,517 --> 00:06:36,956
Then upload a short recording
into a folder named input_audios,

120
00:06:36,956 --> 00:06:38,941
as input-something.mp3.

121
00:06:39,261 --> 00:06:41,094
The transcript keeps that name,

122
00:06:41,094 --> 00:06:43,933
and upload only sends
names starting with input.

123
00:06:44,253 --> 00:06:46,920
Bucket names are global: pick a unique one.

124
00:06:47,240 --> 00:06:48,754
Pause and create yours.

125
00:06:51,100 --> 00:06:55,708
Your bucket only lets in callers
who prove who they are and have permission.

126
00:06:56,028 --> 00:06:59,137
For code,
that identity is a service account,

127
00:06:59,137 --> 00:07:00,380
with one JSON key.

128
00:07:00,750 --> 00:07:04,737
Google's libraries turn that key
into a short-lived token,

129
00:07:04,737 --> 00:07:08,793
and every call to Google's API
doors must pass three gates.

130
00:07:09,033 --> 00:07:14,174
Gate 1 asks who you are: without the key,
the call fails inside your container.

131
00:07:14,494 --> 00:07:18,014
Gate 2 asks what you may do:
the account needs a role.

132
00:07:18,334 --> 00:07:21,257
Gate 3 asks if the API is switched on.

133
00:07:21,500 --> 00:07:26,321
In the console, create a service account,
for example mega-pipeline-sa.

134
00:07:26,641 --> 00:07:30,926
Grant it four roles:
Storage Admin for every component,

135
00:07:30,926 --> 00:07:33,342
Agent Platform User for Gemini,

136
00:07:33,342 --> 00:07:37,393
Cloud Speech Client,
and Cloud Translation API User.

137
00:07:37,633 --> 00:07:39,468
Text-to-Speech needs no role.

138
00:07:39,788 --> 00:07:44,384
A small slip: the generate text
README still says Vertex AI User,

139
00:07:44,384 --> 00:07:46,081
which finds nothing now.

140
00:07:46,317 --> 00:07:50,648
Download a JSON key;
it arrives with a long generated name.

141
00:07:50,968 --> 00:07:56,088
Rename it mega-pipeline.json,
in the secrets folder beside the repo.

142
00:07:56,408 --> 00:07:59,311
Whoever holds it can spend
your project's money:

143
00:07:59,311 --> 00:08:00,823
never commit or paste it.

144
00:08:01,067 --> 00:08:05,355
Next, enable the APIs,
in the console or with gcloud.

145
00:08:05,675 --> 00:08:10,050
Typed exactly as written,
the set project command fails: the shell

146
00:08:10,050 --> 00:08:13,696
reads the placeholder's angle brackets
as redirections.

147
00:08:14,016 --> 00:08:16,896
Type your own project ID, without brackets.

148
00:08:17,133 --> 00:08:21,421
Then the gcloud services enable
command switches on all five.

149
00:08:21,741 --> 00:08:26,682
Our laptop had no Google account,
so gcloud says no active account:

150
00:08:26,682 --> 00:08:29,485
that's the login step from Tutorial 0.

151
00:08:29,805 --> 00:08:33,283
Logged in, it enables them,
and calls bill your project.

152
00:08:33,517 --> 00:08:34,517
Ponder this.

153
00:08:34,818 --> 00:08:38,035
Your key is in place
and every API is enabled,

154
00:08:38,035 --> 00:08:41,112
but you forgot the Agent Platform User role.

155
00:08:41,432 --> 00:08:45,570
Where does generate text fail:
inside your container, or at Google?

156
00:08:54,417 --> 00:08:56,486
At Google, as a permission error.

157
00:08:56,806 --> 00:09:00,305
The key proved who you are;
the role decides what you may do.

158
00:09:00,625 --> 00:09:03,932
New roles and APIs
can take minutes to apply.

159
00:09:04,252 --> 00:09:07,195
Pause, and set up your account, key
and APIs.

160
00:09:09,333 --> 00:09:12,761
Every component runs
through its docker-shell.sh;

161
00:09:12,761 --> 00:09:14,090
let's read it once.

162
00:09:14,410 --> 00:09:17,525
Line 7 names the image,
and the container too.

163
00:09:17,845 --> 00:09:22,389
Lines 8 and 9 build paths from pwd,
the folder you're in.

164
00:09:22,633 --> 00:09:24,852
So run it from the component folder.

165
00:09:25,172 --> 00:09:28,874
Line 10 is the key's path
inside the container;

166
00:09:28,874 --> 00:09:32,340
line 13 names the Docker Hub account, dlops.

167
00:09:32,583 --> 00:09:35,506
The case statement turns your first word
into a mode.

168
00:09:35,826 --> 00:09:37,724
No word: build, then run.

169
00:09:38,044 --> 00:09:40,198
Dev only builds, run only runs,

170
00:09:40,198 --> 00:09:43,740
and prod builds
for two processor types and pushes.

171
00:09:43,983 --> 00:09:47,844
We'll use the default for all five,
then come back to the other three.

172
00:09:48,083 --> 00:09:50,600
Running opens two windows into your laptop.

173
00:09:50,920 --> 00:09:53,701
-v puts the component folder at /app,

174
00:09:53,701 --> 00:09:57,534
and the secrets folder
beside the repo at /secrets.

175
00:09:57,854 --> 00:10:00,124
-e sets an environment variable:

176
00:10:00,124 --> 00:10:03,315
a signpost to the key
for Google's libraries.

177
00:10:03,550 --> 00:10:06,694
As in lecture 3,
the venv lives outside /app,

178
00:10:06,694 --> 00:10:08,649
so the window can't hide it.

179
00:10:08,969 --> 00:10:10,583
The key is never copied:

180
00:10:10,583 --> 00:10:13,811
/secrets shows it only
while the container runs.

181
00:10:14,050 --> 00:10:19,597
Now cd into transcribe audio and run
docker-shell.sh, no argument.

182
00:10:19,917 --> 00:10:24,768
Two steps take nearly all the time:
build step 2, the system packages,

183
00:10:24,768 --> 00:10:29,410
and writing out the image;
a first build also pulls the base image.

184
00:10:29,730 --> 00:10:30,882
Pause and run it.

185
00:10:31,116 --> 00:10:34,664
The full log shows why uv sync is quick:

186
00:10:34,664 --> 00:10:39,277
the image's Python,
3.14.8 at the time of recording,

187
00:10:39,277 --> 00:10:44,066
already satisfies 3.14 or newer,
so uv downloads none.

188
00:10:44,300 --> 00:10:47,239
You're inside, as user app, in /app;

189
00:10:47,239 --> 00:10:51,319
after the @ comes the container ID,
new every run.

190
00:10:51,639 --> 00:10:54,134
The first check, help, matches the README.

191
00:10:54,383 --> 00:10:58,436
Three extra checks inside:
python comes from the venv,

192
00:10:58,436 --> 00:11:02,938
the signpost points to /secrets,
and /secrets is empty here.

193
00:11:03,258 --> 00:11:06,433
In a second terminal,
opened in the same folder,

194
00:11:06,433 --> 00:11:09,210
an extra docker inspect lists both mounts.

195
00:11:09,600 --> 00:11:16,149
Step 5, chmod +x, is harmless:
an extra ls shows it's already executable.

196
00:11:16,469 --> 00:11:19,271
Step 6, on Windows: use Git BASH,

197
00:11:19,271 --> 00:11:25,130
and put winpty in front if Docker says
the input device is not a TTY.

198
00:11:26,467 --> 00:11:28,792
Back in the container, the Quick Reference.

199
00:11:29,112 --> 00:11:33,234
The download step prints its arguments,
then stops:

200
00:11:33,234 --> 00:11:35,201
DefaultCredentialsError:

201
00:11:35,201 --> 00:11:39,054
File /secrets/mega-pipeline.json
was not found.

202
00:11:39,300 --> 00:11:42,137
That's gate 1, failing inside the container.

203
00:11:42,457 --> 00:11:45,434
Usual causes: no key in the secrets folder,

204
00:11:45,434 --> 00:11:49,519
another file name,
or the script run from the wrong folder.

205
00:11:49,767 --> 00:11:52,540
Both transcribe and upload
stop the same way.

206
00:11:52,860 --> 00:11:55,636
With your key, look for the transcript,

207
00:11:55,636 --> 00:11:58,839
then a text file
under text_prompts/group-01,

208
00:11:58,839 --> 00:12:00,668
locally and in the bucket.

209
00:12:00,917 --> 00:12:03,349
Speech-to-Text bills per audio minute,

210
00:12:03,349 --> 00:12:07,110
and this branch keeps only
a long recording's first chunk:

211
00:12:07,110 --> 00:12:08,213
use a short clip.

212
00:12:08,533 --> 00:12:12,032
Pause and run it:
the next component reads its output.

213
00:12:12,267 --> 00:12:16,384
Next: exit, cd into generate text,
and run the script.

214
00:12:16,704 --> 00:12:22,272
Without ffmpeg, its system layer differs:
a fresh build, about 50 seconds.

215
00:12:22,592 --> 00:12:24,150
Help matches the README.

216
00:12:25,434 --> 00:12:27,397
The download fails the same way.

217
00:12:27,717 --> 00:12:32,234
The generate step prints only its arguments
and the word generate,

218
00:12:32,234 --> 00:12:33,946
then exits with status 0.

219
00:12:34,266 --> 00:12:35,695
Did it call Gemini?

220
00:12:42,534 --> 00:12:47,121
No. The download had failed,
so the loop had no prompts to work on.

221
00:12:47,441 --> 00:12:50,427
Exit status 0 doesn't prove it did anything.

222
00:12:50,667 --> 00:12:52,395
The upload needs the key too.

223
00:12:52,715 --> 00:12:57,856
The generate text README adds one step:
a variable picks the Gemini model.

224
00:12:58,176 --> 00:12:59,947
Gemini bills per token.

225
00:13:00,184 --> 00:13:05,325
Next, synthesis_audio_en builds
in 8 seconds, its system layer cached.

226
00:13:05,645 --> 00:13:08,666
We left its cli.py unedited on purpose:

227
00:13:08,666 --> 00:13:12,386
help stops at the assert;
here, the guard works.

228
00:13:12,634 --> 00:13:15,749
Edit it on the laptop:
the window shows it at once.

229
00:13:16,069 --> 00:13:20,988
Now help fails at line 23,
which builds the Google client on import:

230
00:13:20,988 --> 00:13:22,724
even help needs the key.

231
00:13:22,967 --> 00:13:25,314
With your key, you'd see the README's help.

232
00:13:25,634 --> 00:13:26,997
There's no upload step:

233
00:13:26,997 --> 00:13:30,434
Text-to-Speech writes the audio
straight into your bucket.

234
00:13:32,034 --> 00:13:34,597
In translate text: an 8-second build,

235
00:13:34,597 --> 00:13:38,754
help failing at import,
then download, translate and upload.

236
00:13:39,000 --> 00:13:42,691
In synthesis audio, the same:
download, then synthesis.

237
00:13:43,011 --> 00:13:47,022
Pause, and run all five with your key,
checking the bucket after each.

238
00:13:49,467 --> 00:13:50,704
Two small slips.

239
00:13:51,024 --> 00:13:54,985
The translation targeted Spanish,
but the final voice is French:

240
00:13:54,985 --> 00:13:56,656
that's why you switched it.

241
00:13:56,976 --> 00:14:01,958
And the optional ElevenLabs bonus
reads its key from inside /app:

242
00:14:01,958 --> 00:14:03,568
point it at /secrets.

243
00:14:06,417 --> 00:14:09,382
Exit, and cd back into transcribe audio.

244
00:14:09,702 --> 00:14:14,083
Step 2 of How to Run,
docker-shell.sh dev, only builds:

245
00:14:14,083 --> 00:14:15,676
no container starts.

246
00:14:15,996 --> 00:14:19,614
Here, all was cached;
after any change in the folder,

247
00:14:19,614 --> 00:14:22,140
the copy and uv sync steps run again.

248
00:14:22,384 --> 00:14:23,557
What is dev for?

249
00:14:23,877 --> 00:14:25,215
Adding a package:

250
00:14:25,215 --> 00:14:29,308
uv add, inside the container,
updates pyproject.toml

251
00:14:29,308 --> 00:14:34,352
and uv.lock through the window;
then dev bakes it into the image.

252
00:14:35,200 --> 00:14:39,936
Step 3, run, skips the build:
Using local image, because one exists.

253
00:14:40,256 --> 00:14:42,347
Pause and try dev and run yourself.

254
00:14:44,584 --> 00:14:45,833
A small inaccuracy:

255
00:14:45,833 --> 00:14:49,448
the README's overview says
run pulls from the registry.

256
00:14:49,768 --> 00:14:53,662
In fact, any local image of that name wins,
however old;

257
00:14:53,662 --> 00:14:58,322
otherwise it runs the Docker Hub image,
pulled only the first time.

258
00:14:59,567 --> 00:15:00,567
Ponder this.

259
00:15:00,868 --> 00:15:05,562
A teammate has never built this image,
and types docker-shell.sh run.

260
00:15:05,882 --> 00:15:07,375
Which Python do they get?

261
00:15:16,217 --> 00:15:20,676
This extra command deletes our local image,
so we become that teammate.

262
00:15:22,817 --> 00:15:27,702
Local image not found,
so it pulls the dlops image from Docker Hub.

263
00:15:28,022 --> 00:15:30,476
Then, a surprise: Creating a Pipfile.

264
00:15:30,796 --> 00:15:33,740
That's pipenv, this course's tool before uv.

265
00:15:34,984 --> 00:15:36,307
The prompt says root.

266
00:15:36,627 --> 00:15:41,256
Python is 3.8.17,
in an environment pipenv made.

267
00:15:41,576 --> 00:15:45,587
Help still works here,
in the older wording of Python 3.8.

268
00:15:45,907 --> 00:15:47,165
Then type exit.

269
00:15:52,000 --> 00:15:58,332
An extra inspect opens the box:
created on February 18, 2025,

270
00:15:58,332 --> 00:16:03,627
with Python 3.8.17,
starting pipenv shell, as root.

271
00:16:04,667 --> 00:16:09,915
Its build matches an older branch,
pipenv-flexible-workflow, not your recipe.

272
00:16:10,235 --> 00:16:14,416
Your folder slides over its /app:
your code, their environment.

273
00:16:14,736 --> 00:16:18,299
For generate text,
that's the import error from the start:

274
00:16:18,299 --> 00:16:19,835
the first puzzle, solved.

275
00:16:23,184 --> 00:16:26,597
It also left a Pipfile in your folder,
owned by root.

276
00:16:26,917 --> 00:16:30,906
If you tried this, delete it
and run dev to get your own image back.

277
00:16:32,350 --> 00:16:35,155
So build locally, with no argument or dev,

278
00:16:35,155 --> 00:16:39,295
and use run only for images your team pushed
from this branch,

279
00:16:39,295 --> 00:16:41,566
under your own Docker Hub account.

280
00:16:45,000 --> 00:16:46,941
Step 4, prod, publishes.

281
00:16:47,261 --> 00:16:52,765
It recreates a builder named multi-arch,
a build engine for several processor types.

282
00:16:53,085 --> 00:16:57,211
--use makes it current,
and the script never switches back:

283
00:16:57,211 --> 00:16:59,379
docker buildx use default does.

284
00:16:59,617 --> 00:17:04,073
Then one buildx build
makes amd64 and arm64 images

285
00:17:04,073 --> 00:17:06,746
and pushes both under one tag:

286
00:17:06,746 --> 00:17:11,032
as in lecture 2,
an Apple-silicon Mac pulls one,

287
00:17:11,032 --> 00:17:12,907
a cloud VM the other.

288
00:17:13,150 --> 00:17:18,505
Before prod, log in with an access token;
-p leaves it in your shell history.

289
00:17:18,825 --> 00:17:21,470
Set the Docker username
in the script to your own.

290
00:17:21,790 --> 00:17:25,118
Don't run prod yet:
first, check what it would publish.

291
00:17:25,367 --> 00:17:26,367
Here's why.

292
00:17:26,668 --> 00:17:31,426
In the switched lecture 3 clone,
the old key still sits in transcribe audio.

293
00:17:31,746 --> 00:17:33,197
Build there with dev,

294
00:17:33,197 --> 00:17:36,514
and an extra command
lists the new image's /app:

295
00:17:36,514 --> 00:17:37,740
the key is inside.

296
00:17:37,983 --> 00:17:40,202
The copy line takes the whole folder.

297
00:17:40,522 --> 00:17:43,027
The .gitignore keeps secrets out of Git,

298
00:17:43,027 --> 00:17:46,346
not out of images,
and no branch has a .dockerignore.

299
00:17:46,666 --> 00:17:48,799
Prod would push your key to Docker Hub.

300
00:17:49,033 --> 00:17:52,724
So the README's promise holds for Git,
not for images.

301
00:17:53,044 --> 00:17:56,022
Delete any secrets folder
inside a component,

302
00:17:56,022 --> 00:17:58,405
add a .dockerignore listing secrets,

303
00:17:58,405 --> 00:18:01,449
and if a key was ever pushed,
delete that key.

304
00:18:01,769 --> 00:18:06,548
Prod is optional: after the fix,
run it only if your team publishes.

305
00:18:06,868 --> 00:18:08,340
Pause and check yours.

306
00:18:10,083 --> 00:18:11,576
Clean-up for part 1.

307
00:18:11,896 --> 00:18:15,374
Exit each container; --rm deletes it.

308
00:18:15,694 --> 00:18:19,493
The transcribe image alone
is 1.48 gigabytes:

309
00:18:19,493 --> 00:18:25,486
remove all five with docker image rm,
plus any dlops images you pulled.

310
00:18:25,733 --> 00:18:28,556
In the cloud,
the bucket bills a little storage

311
00:18:28,556 --> 00:18:29,758
until you delete it,

312
00:18:29,758 --> 00:18:34,138
when the course is over; the key works
until you delete it in the console.

313
00:18:34,458 --> 00:18:37,979
In part 2:
containers that talk to each other directly.

314
00:18:40,117 --> 00:18:43,466
In part 1,
containers only left files in a bucket.

315
00:18:43,786 --> 00:18:48,232
Port toy makes two containers talk directly,
and needs only Docker:

316
00:18:48,232 --> 00:18:52,613
a gateway serves a web page
and forwards your text to a processor,

317
00:18:52,613 --> 00:18:54,538
which turns it into capitals.

318
00:18:54,784 --> 00:18:57,943
Think of each container
as its own little computer,

319
00:18:57,943 --> 00:19:01,227
with its own localhost
and its own full set of ports.

320
00:19:01,547 --> 00:19:05,856
Two containers can both use
port 9100 without any clash.

321
00:19:06,176 --> 00:19:09,115
The lecture calls this a network namespace:

322
00:19:09,115 --> 00:19:12,192
each container is isolated
until you wire it.

323
00:19:12,434 --> 00:19:16,051
A network you create joins them
like a small home network,

324
00:19:16,051 --> 00:19:19,794
with a phone book
that turns container names into addresses.

325
00:19:20,114 --> 00:19:22,226
Docker's default network has none.

326
00:19:22,467 --> 00:19:26,819
Prerequisites: docker run hello-world checks
that Docker is running.

327
00:19:27,139 --> 00:19:30,424
It pulls a tiny image and prints
Hello from Docker!

328
00:19:30,667 --> 00:19:32,907
Step 1 sets the app up by hand.

329
00:19:33,227 --> 00:19:35,951
From your workspace folder, clone the code:

330
00:19:35,951 --> 00:19:39,499
the README says clone or download
but prints no command.

331
00:19:39,819 --> 00:19:41,547
Then cd into port toy.

332
00:19:41,867 --> 00:19:43,126
Pause and run them.

333
00:19:43,367 --> 00:19:46,802
Two extra checks:
the files match the README's tree,

334
00:19:46,802 --> 00:19:48,544
a gateway and a processor,

335
00:19:48,544 --> 00:19:51,559
and git log shows
the commit this video pins.

336
00:19:51,800 --> 00:19:54,211
Next, docker network create toynet.

337
00:19:54,531 --> 00:19:56,707
Docker prints the new network's ID:

338
00:19:56,707 --> 00:19:59,630
that's the rail both containers
will plug into.

339
00:19:59,867 --> 00:20:03,835
From the README's handy commands,
docker network ls lists it,

340
00:20:03,835 --> 00:20:06,438
beside Docker's three built-in networks.

341
00:20:06,758 --> 00:20:08,336
Pause, and create yours.

342
00:20:09,383 --> 00:20:11,090
Next, build two images.

343
00:20:11,410 --> 00:20:14,858
The two Dockerfiles are almost identical:

344
00:20:14,858 --> 00:20:18,642
a slim Python 3.14 image,
plus curl from apt.

345
00:20:18,962 --> 00:20:23,868
Each copies its code and runs it with -u,
so prints reach the logs at once.

346
00:20:24,116 --> 00:20:27,913
The processor builds first,
and its apt layer takes a few seconds.

347
00:20:28,233 --> 00:20:31,434
Your first build also pulls
the Python base image.

348
00:20:31,683 --> 00:20:32,808
Then the gateway:

349
00:20:32,808 --> 00:20:36,445
Docker reuses the processor's apt layer
from the cache,

350
00:20:36,445 --> 00:20:39,576
because the two files match up
to the copy line.

351
00:20:39,896 --> 00:20:41,496
It's done in under a second.

352
00:20:42,483 --> 00:20:48,264
Then start the processor on toynet,
with PORT set to 9200, and no -p.

353
00:20:48,584 --> 00:20:54,451
It listens on 9200 inside its own box,
but nothing on your laptop leads to it.

354
00:20:54,700 --> 00:20:56,108
Then start the gateway.

355
00:20:56,428 --> 00:20:57,866
-p publishes a port:

356
00:20:57,866 --> 00:21:03,617
it opens a street door on your laptop
that leads to a room inside the container.

357
00:21:03,937 --> 00:21:09,377
The order is IP address,
host port, container port:

358
00:21:09,377 --> 00:21:15,006
127.0.0.1,
meaning this laptop only, then door 8090,

359
00:21:15,006 --> 00:21:16,630
then room 9100.

360
00:21:18,116 --> 00:21:21,897
The two -e flags tell the gateway
where to call:

361
00:21:21,897 --> 00:21:24,260
the name processor, port 9200.

362
00:21:24,580 --> 00:21:27,266
Lines 13 and 14 of its code read them;

363
00:21:27,266 --> 00:21:30,447
the values in quotes there
are only defaults.

364
00:21:30,683 --> 00:21:32,667
Now check docker logs for each.

365
00:21:32,987 --> 00:21:39,750
The processor listens on 9200,
internal only; the gateway listens on 9100.

366
00:21:40,070 --> 00:21:41,499
Pause and start both.

367
00:21:43,733 --> 00:21:47,609
To finish step 1,
open localhost:8090 in your browser,

368
00:21:47,609 --> 00:21:49,834
type a message, and click Send.

369
00:21:50,154 --> 00:21:53,701
The reply comes back in capitals,
with three port numbers:

370
00:21:53,701 --> 00:21:57,247
where the gateway sent from,
where the processor listened,

371
00:21:57,247 --> 00:21:59,754
and where it saw the message arrive from.

372
00:22:00,000 --> 00:22:02,055
We recorded without a browser,

373
00:22:02,055 --> 00:22:05,137
so curl sent the same request
the page sends;

374
00:22:05,137 --> 00:22:06,826
this is the reply it got.

375
00:22:07,550 --> 00:22:11,347
The processor listened on 9200,
the destination.

376
00:22:11,667 --> 00:22:15,998
But every connection has two ends,
so the gateway's side needs a port too.

377
00:22:16,318 --> 00:22:20,942
Its operating system picked
a temporary one, 55620,

378
00:22:20,942 --> 00:22:23,571
and stapled it to the packet.

379
00:22:23,816 --> 00:22:26,222
The processor saw the same number arrive,

380
00:22:26,222 --> 00:22:28,979
because nothing
rewrites it inside the network.

381
00:22:29,299 --> 00:22:36,062
Line 38 of the gateway reads its own port;
line 32 of the processor reads the sender's.

382
00:22:36,300 --> 00:22:41,228
Send again, and the number changes:
a new connection, a new temporary port.

383
00:22:41,548 --> 00:22:42,806
Yours will differ.

384
00:22:43,050 --> 00:22:44,278
One more detail:

385
00:22:44,278 --> 00:22:48,959
the page's diagram shows 8090
by reading its own address bar.

386
00:22:49,279 --> 00:22:52,629
The container can't know
which door on your laptop you gave it.

387
00:22:52,949 --> 00:22:55,700
Pause, send a message,
and leave both running.

388
00:22:56,750 --> 00:22:58,435
Step 2 checks the ports.

389
00:22:58,755 --> 00:23:05,505
docker ps shows the gateway's mapping,
host side first:

390
00:23:05,505 --> 00:23:09,187
127.0.0.1, port 8090, to 9100.

391
00:23:09,507 --> 00:23:14,051
The processor's PORTS column is empty:
listening, but not published.

392
00:23:14,300 --> 00:23:18,673
An extra docker ps with three columns
matches the README's sample.

393
00:23:20,067 --> 00:23:23,822
What does the 127.0.0.1 do?

394
00:23:24,142 --> 00:23:28,750
This extra run leaves it out,
asking for door 8090 on every address.

395
00:23:29,070 --> 00:23:33,998
Docker refuses, port is already allocated:
our gateway holds 8090.

396
00:23:34,318 --> 00:23:37,155
The container was still created,
so remove it.

397
00:23:37,400 --> 00:23:42,143
With door 8091 it works,
and the mapping shows 0.0.0.0:

398
00:23:42,143 --> 00:23:47,576
every address, so other machines
on your network could connect.

399
00:23:47,896 --> 00:23:48,896
Remove it.

400
00:23:49,176 --> 00:23:55,427
That's why the README writes 127.0.0.1:
this laptop only.

401
00:23:55,667 --> 00:23:59,784
From the laptop,
curl on 8090 returns the gateway's page.

402
00:24:00,104 --> 00:24:04,991
curl on 9200 can't connect:
your laptop has no door to it,

403
00:24:04,991 --> 00:24:08,445
unless something else of yours uses 9200.

404
00:24:08,683 --> 00:24:12,275
From inside the gateway,
docker exec runs curl,

405
00:24:12,275 --> 00:24:15,638
asking for the name processor, on port 9200.

406
00:24:15,958 --> 00:24:17,473
The answer: ok.

407
00:24:17,717 --> 00:24:18,717
Ponder this.

408
00:24:19,018 --> 00:24:22,325
The same command
with localhost instead of processor.

409
00:24:22,645 --> 00:24:23,669
What answers?

410
00:24:30,517 --> 00:24:31,517
Nothing.

411
00:24:31,648 --> 00:24:35,673
Inside the gateway,
localhost is the gateway itself,

412
00:24:35,673 --> 00:24:38,304
and nothing there listens on 9200.

413
00:24:38,624 --> 00:24:42,656
That's the second puzzle:
each container has its own localhost.

414
00:24:42,976 --> 00:24:48,522
An extra check: on 9100, its own port,
localhost returns the page.

415
00:24:48,767 --> 00:24:50,324
So how did the name work?

416
00:24:50,644 --> 00:24:52,166
From the handy commands,

417
00:24:52,166 --> 00:24:55,908
docker network inspect shows an address
for each container,

418
00:24:55,908 --> 00:24:58,665
from toynet's own address range, its subnet.

419
00:24:59,767 --> 00:25:04,222
Each container asks
Docker's built-in phone book,

420
00:25:04,222 --> 00:25:09,132
at 127.0.0.11,
which turns names into those addresses.

421
00:25:09,452 --> 00:25:13,143
Addresses can change
when a container restarts; names stay.

422
00:25:13,383 --> 00:25:15,264
Another handy command,

423
00:25:15,264 --> 00:25:19,454
docker exec -it gateway sh,
opens a shell inside;

424
00:25:19,454 --> 00:25:22,236
its hostname is the container ID.

425
00:25:22,556 --> 00:25:27,783
One aside: the processor image has curl too,
though the README implies it doesn't.

426
00:25:28,103 --> 00:25:30,151
Pause, and test from both sides.

427
00:25:31,800 --> 00:25:33,720
Step 3 changes the ports.

428
00:25:34,040 --> 00:25:42,957
First the street door: remove the gateway,
and start it again on 127.0.0.1, port 8095.

429
00:25:43,277 --> 00:25:45,880
The room inside stays 9100.

430
00:25:46,117 --> 00:25:48,400
The page works on 8095.

431
00:25:48,720 --> 00:25:54,352
Two extra checks: 8090 no longer connects,
and docker ps shows the new mapping.

432
00:25:54,672 --> 00:25:56,677
Pause and move your gateway's door.

433
00:25:59,167 --> 00:26:00,170
Why do this?

434
00:26:00,490 --> 00:26:04,074
Another app on your laptop
might already hold 8090.

435
00:26:04,394 --> 00:26:09,428
Doors on the laptop must be unique;
rooms inside containers never collide.

436
00:26:09,667 --> 00:26:14,510
A small slip: the gateway's log
still says open localhost:8090.

437
00:26:14,830 --> 00:26:17,411
That text is hard-coded in line 53.

438
00:26:17,731 --> 00:26:20,333
Trust docker ps and the page instead.

439
00:26:20,583 --> 00:26:26,215
Next, the processor's port: remove it,
and start it with PORT set to 9300.

440
00:26:26,535 --> 00:26:29,671
The README says:
send a message, and it should fail.

441
00:26:30,067 --> 00:26:31,560
It does, but quietly.

442
00:26:31,880 --> 00:26:35,742
The page has no error handling,
so nothing on it changes;

443
00:26:35,742 --> 00:26:40,349
the captured request got only curl's error,
Empty reply from server.

444
00:26:40,583 --> 00:26:44,122
An extra docker logs
on the gateway shows why:

445
00:26:44,122 --> 00:26:46,507
it found the processor by name,

446
00:26:46,507 --> 00:26:51,292
but the connection was refused:
nobody listens on 9200 anymore.

447
00:26:51,612 --> 00:26:55,026
A wrong name fails differently:
see the exercises.

448
00:26:55,267 --> 00:26:56,267
Ponder this.

449
00:26:56,568 --> 00:27:00,387
Which one setting fixes it,
and does any image need rebuilding?

450
00:27:07,233 --> 00:27:09,025
The gateway's processor port.

451
00:27:09,345 --> 00:27:15,724
Remove the gateway and start it with 9300,
back on 8090, and the message goes through.

452
00:27:16,044 --> 00:27:19,478
No image was rebuilt:
it's only configuration.

453
00:27:19,798 --> 00:27:21,249
Pause and fix yours.

454
00:27:22,233 --> 00:27:23,967
The two settings must agree:

455
00:27:23,967 --> 00:27:27,311
the processor's PORT
and the gateway's processor port.

456
00:27:27,550 --> 00:27:32,286
Finally, clean up before Compose:
remove both containers, then the network.

457
00:27:32,606 --> 00:27:36,361
An extra check shows only
hello-world's old container left.

458
00:27:36,681 --> 00:27:39,518
Skip this, and Compose hits a name conflict.

459
00:27:39,838 --> 00:27:41,139
Pause and clean up.

460
00:27:44,433 --> 00:27:49,660
Step 4: Docker Compose, which builds
and starts the whole app from one YAML file.

461
00:27:49,980 --> 00:27:54,460
The README shows an excerpt;
open the whole docker-compose.yml.

462
00:27:54,780 --> 00:27:58,150
Almost every flag you typed
by hand has a home in it.

463
00:27:58,400 --> 00:28:02,987
--name becomes container name,
and the build command becomes build.

464
00:28:03,307 --> 00:28:06,340
-p becomes ports, -e becomes environment,

465
00:28:06,340 --> 00:28:10,112
and the network is named
at the bottom of the file.

466
00:28:12,000 --> 00:28:13,155
Two keys are new:

467
00:28:13,155 --> 00:28:16,823
the volumes key mounts the gateway's folder
into /app,

468
00:28:16,823 --> 00:28:20,171
and the depends on key
starts the processor first.

469
00:28:20,491 --> 00:28:24,565
All of step 1 collapses into one command:
docker compose up.

470
00:28:24,800 --> 00:28:25,800
Run it.

471
00:28:25,952 --> 00:28:29,244
Compose builds both images,
all from the cache,

472
00:28:29,244 --> 00:28:33,866
and names them after the folder
and the service: port_toy-gateway.

473
00:28:34,116 --> 00:28:37,038
Then it creates the network
and both containers,

474
00:28:37,038 --> 00:28:40,751
and prints both banners,
each line prefixed with its service.

475
00:28:41,071 --> 00:28:42,223
Pause and run it.

476
00:28:42,466 --> 00:28:44,279
Open the page: it works.

477
00:28:44,599 --> 00:28:47,725
In a second terminal,
cd into port toy first:

478
00:28:47,725 --> 00:28:50,850
Compose reads the file
in the current folder.

479
00:28:51,170 --> 00:28:54,306
There, docker compose ps
shows the same mapping.

480
00:28:54,550 --> 00:28:58,475
An extra network ls shows
that Compose made toynet,

481
00:28:58,475 --> 00:29:02,400
and compose exec reaches the processor
by name: ok.

482
00:29:02,766 --> 00:29:07,310
The README's table pairs each by-hand
command with its Compose version.

483
00:29:07,630 --> 00:29:10,937
In the first terminal,
your requests now show in the log.

484
00:29:11,183 --> 00:29:14,305
The README stops the app
at the end of step 4;

485
00:29:14,305 --> 00:29:17,562
we stop it now, in the foreground:
press Ctrl+C.

486
00:29:17,882 --> 00:29:22,042
Compose says it's stopping gracefully,
but it takes about 20 seconds.

487
00:29:25,250 --> 00:29:27,836
Python, each container's first process,

488
00:29:27,836 --> 00:29:32,013
ignores the polite stop signal
unless it's written to catch it.

489
00:29:32,333 --> 00:29:35,874
So Docker waits 10 seconds per container,
then kills it.

490
00:29:38,783 --> 00:29:41,578
Exit code 137 means killed.

491
00:29:41,898 --> 00:29:43,450
docker compose ps -a:

492
00:29:43,450 --> 00:29:46,996
both containers exited,
but they're still there.

493
00:29:47,233 --> 00:29:51,495
docker compose down removes the containers
and the network,

494
00:29:51,495 --> 00:29:53,590
and nothing is left in ps -a.

495
00:29:53,910 --> 00:29:56,236
Ctrl+C stops; down removes.

496
00:29:56,556 --> 00:29:58,497
Pause, and run the whole cycle.

497
00:29:59,533 --> 00:30:01,268
Back to the README's order:

498
00:30:01,268 --> 00:30:05,314
up -d starts the app in the background
and returns your prompt.

499
00:30:05,550 --> 00:30:06,894
Two handy commands.

500
00:30:07,214 --> 00:30:10,815
docker compose logs -f
follows the gateway's log;

501
00:30:10,815 --> 00:30:13,166
Ctrl+C stops only the following.

502
00:30:13,486 --> 00:30:16,888
docker compose exec
gateway sh opens a shell,

503
00:30:16,888 --> 00:30:20,440
where the environment
shows processor and 9200.

504
00:30:22,083 --> 00:30:25,859
Next, the README says
to repeat the port experiments in the file.

505
00:30:26,179 --> 00:30:32,878
Change 8090 to 8095 and run up -d:
Compose recreates only the gateway.

506
00:30:33,116 --> 00:30:37,447
The page answers on 8095;
change it back and apply again.

507
00:30:40,300 --> 00:30:42,647
We added a comment to the processor's code.

508
00:30:42,967 --> 00:30:46,913
Changed code that's copied
into an image needs --build.

509
00:30:47,233 --> 00:30:51,478
Only the copy layer is new,
and only the processor is recreated.

510
00:30:51,716 --> 00:30:56,184
The gateway's folder is mounted,
and it rereads its page on each load:

511
00:30:56,184 --> 00:30:59,311
edits show on the next reload,
without a rebuild.

512
00:31:00,166 --> 00:31:04,731
To finish, docker compose down;
nothing is left in ps -a.

513
00:31:05,051 --> 00:31:08,568
The images remain,
sharing about 200 megabytes;

514
00:31:08,568 --> 00:31:10,513
remove them to free space.

515
00:31:10,833 --> 00:31:12,326
Port toy costs nothing.

516
00:31:12,646 --> 00:31:14,097
Pause and shut it down.

517
00:31:14,333 --> 00:31:17,106
To test yourself, pause after each question.

518
00:31:17,426 --> 00:31:20,442
One:
which files define a mega-pipeline image,

519
00:31:20,442 --> 00:31:23,720
and what does run use
when there's no local image?

520
00:31:23,966 --> 00:31:27,165
Two: which three gates must a call
to your bucket pass,

521
00:31:27,165 --> 00:31:28,851
and where does each one fail?

522
00:31:29,171 --> 00:31:32,329
Three: how does a git-ignored key
end up in an image,

523
00:31:32,329 --> 00:31:33,460
and what's the fix?

524
00:31:33,700 --> 00:31:40,655
Four: -p 127.0.0.1:8095:9100.

525
00:31:40,975 --> 00:31:44,005
Which number does the browser use,

526
00:31:44,005 --> 00:31:49,529
which does the gateway bind,
and what does 127.0.0.1 restrict?

527
00:31:49,849 --> 00:31:55,012
Five: inside the gateway, what is localhost,
and which name reaches the processor?

528
00:31:55,250 --> 00:32:00,155
Next lecture, a command-line container
calls a database container by name

529
00:32:00,155 --> 00:32:03,561
on a Compose network,
with the same secrets mount:

530
00:32:03,561 --> 00:32:05,127
parts 1 and 2 together.

531
00:32:05,447 --> 00:32:07,495
Answers are in the exercises.

